Under DORA, agreements with ICT third-party service providers must be in writing and clearly allocate the rights and obligations of both parties, with the full contract including the service level agreements in one document or another downloadable, durable and accessible format [1].

At a minimum, the contractual arrangements must include:  
- a clear and complete description of the functions and ICT services to be provided, including whether subcontracting of critical/important services is permitted and on what conditions [1];  
- the locations where the functions/services will be provided and where data will be processed/stored, plus advance notice of any change in those locations [1];  
- provisions on availability, authenticity, integrity and confidentiality of data, including personal data [1];  
- where relevant, conditions for the provider’s participation in the financial entity’s ICT security awareness programmes and digital operational resilience training [2].

For ICT services supporting critical or important functions, the contract must also include, in addition to the above:  
- full service level descriptions with precise quantitative and qualitative performance targets [3];  
- notice periods and reporting obligations, including notification of developments that could materially affect the provider’s ability to deliver the services [3];  
- requirements to implement and test business contingency plans and maintain appropriate ICT security measures, tools and policies [3];  
- provisions ensuring access, recovery and return of personal and non-personal data in case of insolvency, resolution, discontinuation, or termination [3];  
- service level descriptions, assistance obligations in the event of an ICT incident, cooperation with competent and resolution authorities, termination rights and minimum notice periods, and training-participation conditions [3];  
- details on the scope, procedures and frequency of inspections and audits [4];  
- exit strategies, including a mandatory adequate transition period and migration support to another provider or in-house solutions [4].

The documents also say that, when negotiating these arrangements, financial entities and ICT third-party service providers should consider standard contractual clauses developed by public authorities for specific services [4].
